parts.text:
Good. Now let me think about whether the frontend will actually work with the `#/porsequencia?id=6` redirect and auto-selection. There's one subtlety: AngularJS `$location.search().id` in `carregarSequencias` success callback. At the time the controller is instantiated, the URL already has `?id=6`. Good. But the route `#/porsequencia?id=6` — Angular's `$routeProvider` matches path `/porsequencia` ignoring query params. Good.

Another subtlety: `$location.search('id', null)` in `limparPesquisa` — in Angular 1.4.9, `$location.search()` with `null` removes. Yes.

Now, one issue: The `#/porsequencia` template has `<df-hex ... ng-click="Sequencia.limparPesquisa()">` for the selected sequence. If the user clicks a chord in the selected sequence, it clears the search. Fine, existing behavior.

Now, another consideration: In `sequencia.js`, `vm.temAcorde` compares `.acorde` strings. The template calls `Sequencia.temAcorde(acordeon)` where `acordeon` is an object. I changed the signature to expect a string, but the template passes an object! Let me check: In `sequencia.html` line 7:
```
<df-hex ... acorde-checked="Sequencia.temAcorde(acordeon)" ...>
```
`acordeon` is the object `{acorde, cor}`. My `temAcorde(acorde)` expects a string and compares `vm.minhaSequencia.acordes[i].acorde === acorde`. That would compare string to object → false always. Bug!

In the original code, `temAcorde(acorde)` also expected a string but was passed `acordeon` (object) — wait, the original:
```js
vm.temAcorde = function(acorde) {
  if (vm.minhaSequencia.acordes) {
    return (vm.minhaSequencia.acordes.indexOf(acorde) > -1);
  } else {
    return false;
  }
}
```
And the template passed `acordeon` (object). So it checked if the object reference is in `minhaSequencia.acordes`. Since `vm.minhaSequencia` is set to the same `sequencia` object that's in `sequenciasFamosas`, the objects are the same references, so `indexOf(acordeon)` works! That's how highlighting works in the original.

So I should keep `temAcorde` accepting the object and using `indexOf`. Let me fix `sequencia.js` to match original semantics:

```js
vm.temAcorde = function(acordeon) {
  if (vm.minhaSequencia.acordes) {
    return (vm.minhaSequencia.acordes.indexOf(acordeon) > -1);
  }
  return false;
}
```

Since `vm.minhaSequencia` is assigned the same object from `sequenciasFamosas`, the references match. Good. Let me fix.

Also in `cadastra-seqfamosa.js`, `temAcorde(acordeon)` uses `indexOf(acordeon)` which works because the selected array holds the same object references from the groups. Good.

Let me fix sequencia.js.

